## Deployments ##
> A deployments in kubernetes is used to manage and run your application pods.
Deployment
    ↓
ReplicaSet
    ↓
Pods
    ↓
Container
    ↓
Application

> for example :- if you want 3 nginx Pods, you don't normally create 3 Pods manually. 
> You create a Deployment:
nginx-deployment
       ↓
   ReplicaSet
       ↓
 ┌─────┼─────┐
Pod-1 Pod-2 Pod-3

> The Deployment continuously makes sure the desired number of Pods are running.


> <<<<<<>>>>>> {why we use deployment} <<<<<<>>>>>>

> without deployment:

You create Pod
     ↓
Pod crashes
     ↓
You manually create another Pod 

> with deployment 

Deployment
     ↓
Pod crashes
     ↓
Kubernetes automatically creates replacement Pod 

>**** deployment also provide ****

Scaling — increase/decrease Pods
Rolling updates — update application without stopping everything
Rollback — go back to previous version
Self-healing — maintain desired Pod count
Version/revision history

***************************************************************************************************************

### Basic deployment YAML ###

apiVersion: apps/v1 --->>> (which api version we're using)
kind: Deployment   --->>> (tells k8s :- i want to create deployment)

metadata:
  name: nginx-deployment -->> (deployment name)
  namespace: akash -->> (where the deployment create)

spec:
  replicas: 3 -->> (keep 3 pods running)

  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate    ---->>>> for update image
    rollingUpdate:
      maxUnavailable: 1
      maxSurge: 1

  selector:  -->> { tells the Deployment which Pods it should manage.}
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx

  template: -->> { these level must match the selector }
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx

    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx -->> {container-name}
          image: nginx:1.25 -->>(docker-image)
          ports:
            - containerPort: 80 -->>(The application/container listens on port 80.)

***************************************************************************************************************** 

>> **** BASIC DEPLOYMENT COMMANDS **** <<

    1. check deployments :- kubectl get deployment -n namespace
    2. check replicaset :- kubectl get replicaset -n namespace
    
>   scale deployment :- suppose you currently have replicas: 3,
>   you can increase it 5 using :- kubectl scale deployment nginx-deployment --replicas=5 -n namespace
>   you can also scale down using:- kubectl scale deployment nginx-deployment --replicas=2 -n namespace


> *** update the application ***

    suppose your currently image :- image: nginx:1.25, and you want :- nginx:1.26
> command to update :- kubectl set image deployment nginx=nginx:1.26 -n namespace    

> *** rolling update commands ***

    >> command to check rollout status : kubectl rollout status deployment deployment-name
>>  to check rollout history : kubectl rollout history deployment deployment-name

>>  to check rollout history with specifc revision: kubectl rollout history deployment deployment-name --revision revision-number

>> to rollout deployment via imperative approach : kubectl set image deployment myapp-deployment nginx-container=nginx:1.15   
>> to update change-cause in rollout history: kubectl annotate deployment deployment-name kubernetes.io/change-cause="Need to mention cause here"
>> to add change-use in rollout without annotate: kubectl set image deployment myapp-deployment nginx-container=1.15 --record=true
>> command to undo rollout: kubectl rollout undo deployment deployment-name 

>> to undo rollout to specific version: kubectl rollout undo deployment deployment-name --to-revision=revision_number

>> command to pause rollout: kubectl rollout pause depolyment deployment-name

>> command to resume rollout: kubectl rollout resume deployment deployment-name


    